      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>If you have ever asked what a <strong>Care Inspectorate grade</strong> actually means, or searched for something like what does grade 4 mean, this page is the plain-terms answer. The Care Inspectorate grades Scottish care services on a six-point scale for each key question it evaluates. Six is the highest and one is the lowest. The descriptions here are general, drawn from the published scale, to help you interpret a grade or work towards one.</p><p>Grades and their exact definitions are set by the Care Inspectorate and updated over time, so always refer to the Care Inspectorate's current published definitions for the precise wording. Treat this page as general information, not regulatory advice.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>The Care Inspectorate uses a six-point grade scale: 6 excellent, 5 very good, 4 good, 3 adequate, 2 weak, 1 unsatisfactory.</li><li>A grade is given per key question, such as how good the care and support or the leadership is, not as a single overall mark.</li><li>Grade 4 (good) and above broadly means strengths outweigh weaknesses; grade 3 (adequate) is the point where improvement is clearly needed.</li><li>Grades 2 and 1 signal significant or major weaknesses that require action, sometimes urgently.</li><li>What moves a grade is evidence of good outcomes for people, honest self-evaluation, and visible action on weaknesses.</li><li>Always refer to the Care Inspectorate's current published definitions; this page describes the scale in general terms only.</li></ul>"
