      "html": "<p>Each grade broadly describes the balance of strengths and weaknesses a service shows against a key question. The precise definitions are the Care Inspectorate's; always refer to its current published wording.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Grade</th><th>Word</th><th>What it broadly means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>6</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Sector-leading practice. Outcomes for people are consistently of a very high standard and the service is an example others could learn from.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Very good</td><td>Major strengths and very few areas for improvement. Strong, high-quality practice with good outcomes for people.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Good</td><td>Important strengths that clearly outweigh weaknesses. Practice is sound, with some areas that could still be improved.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Adequate</td><td>Strengths only just outweigh weaknesses. Acceptable, but improvement is needed and weaknesses must be addressed.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Weak</td><td>Significant weaknesses that outweigh strengths and must be addressed. Outcomes for people are at risk of being compromised.</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Unsatisfactory</td><td>Major weaknesses requiring urgent and prioritised action. Outcomes for people are, or are likely to be, seriously compromised.</td></tr></tbody></table>"
