      "heading": "What does grade 4 mean?",
      "html": "<p>Grade 4 is good. It broadly means the service has important strengths that clearly outweigh its weaknesses. Practice is sound and outcomes for people are positive, but there are still areas that could be improved. It is a solid grade that most services are comfortable to hold, and it sits above the adequate line, so it signals that the fundamentals are in place rather than merely acceptable.</p><p>To move from good to very good, a service typically needs to show more consistent, higher-quality practice across the whole key question, stronger evidence of good outcomes for people, and self-evaluation that is honest and acted upon. To slip from good to adequate, weaknesses grow until they only just outweigh the strengths. The Care Inspectorate's current published definitions are the authority on where exactly the line falls.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Up or down",
      "heading": "What typically moves a grade",
      "html": "<p>Grades are not awarded on paperwork alone. What tends to move a service up is clear evidence of good outcomes for the people using the service, a staff team that is skilled and well supported, a setting that supports wellbeing, and leadership that self-evaluates honestly and acts on what it finds. A service that knows its own weaknesses and can show the improvement work behind each one is treated very differently from one that grades itself generously and cannot back it up.</p><p>What tends to move a service down is the opposite: weaknesses that are not being addressed, outcomes that are slipping, gaps in staffing, training or safe recruitment, and self-evaluation that is either absent or not honest. The single most useful thing a service can do is run the inspector's framework on itself first, so any gap is found and closed before an inspection rather than discovered during one.</p>"
